It's possible that the server's use of `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` is preventing you from including an Authorization header in your request. When a server uses a wildcard origin (`*`) in its CORS configuration, it allows any website to access resources on the server, regardless of whether they have been authorized or not. This can pose security risks and may not be ideal for applications that require authentication and authorization.

To allow clients to include an Authorization header in their requests, you need to specify the allowed origins as well as the HTTP methods and headers that are allowed for each origin. Here's an example of how you could configure CORS for an Express.js application:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const cors = require('cors');

const app = express();
app.use(cors({
  origin: ['http://example.com', 'https://another-site.com'], // list of allowed origins
  optionsSuccessStatus: 204, // return empty response on preflight requests
}));

// ... Rest of your application code here ...
```

In this example, we're using the `cors` middleware to configure CORS. We're specifying two allowed origins (`http://example.com` and `https://another-site.com`), and we're setting `optionsSuccessStatus` to `204` so that preflight requests don't return a 200 status code. This prevents the "Preflight Request Failed" error that you might see when making cross-origin requests.

With this configuration, you should be able to make requests to your server with an Authorization header, provided that the client has already authenticated and obtained a valid token. If you're still having issues, you may want to check the server logs or network traffic to see if there are any errors being reported.

